title: Genesis 14 – Melchizedek
description: A verse-by-verse study of Genesis 14: Abram rescues Lot, Melchizedek king of Salem brings forth bread and wine, and Abram refuses the spoils of Sodom.
output: lessons/genesis/genesis-14-melchizedek.html

# Genesis series pattern (Genesis 1-5). Slide text is the author's own sentences from the Genesis 14 study
# (posts 6812-6815). Fixed: "his does" -> "his foes"; "Issac" -> "Isaac".

=== Introduction
--- Genesis Chapter 14 [title]
overline: Young Adult Sunday School • Genesis Series
heading: Genesis 14: Melchizedek
sub: The War of the Kings • Lot Rescued • Bread and Wine
refs: Genesis 14:1-24
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

=== The war of the kings
--- Four Kings Against Five
kicker: vv. 1–4
sub: In the Thirteenth Year They Rebelled
scripture: Genesis 14:1-4

--- The Kings Smote the Land
kicker: vv. 5–7
sub: The Rephaims, the Zuzims, and the Emims
scripture: Genesis 14:5-7

--- The Vale of Siddim
kicker: vv. 8–10
sub: The Kings of Sodom and Gomorrah Fled
scripture: Genesis 14:8-10

--- They Took Lot
kicker: vv. 11–12
sub: Who Dwelt in Sodom
scripture: Genesis 14:11-12 | mark: they took Lot, Abram’s brother’s son, who dwelt in Sodom
p: Lot, who we saw pitch his tent toward Sodom, is punished, spoiled and taken into captivity by the other kings’ armies. [[Genesis 13:12]]

--- Abram Armed His Trained Servants
kicker: vv. 13–16
sub: He Brought Back His Brother Lot
scripture: Genesis 14:13-16 | mark: he armed his trained servants; brought again his brother Lot
p: Abram, seeing his brother in Christ, his nephew Lot, taken, seeks Lot’s rescue. He is not part of the war of the kings itself. But he is the finisher of it, with God’s hand defeating his foes and freeing Lot and his people and possessions.

=== King of Salem
--- Melchizedek, King of Salem
kicker: vv. 17–20
sub: Bread and Wine, and the Priest of the Most High God
scripture: Genesis 14:17-20 | mark: brought forth bread and wine: and he was the priest of the most high God; he gave him tithes of all
p: Melchizedek, king of peace, priest of the {Lord} God, Jehovah. God the Father.

--- A Priest For Ever
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 14:18
fig: Psalm 110:4 | Psalm 110:4
fig: Hebrews 7:3 | Hebrews 7:3 | As always, the New Testament is the best study guide for the Old Testament. Here Paul gives us understanding into who exactly Melchizedek, king of Salem, priest of the most high God, was, or at least pictures: Jesus Christ. [[Hebrews 7:1-3]]

--- A Priest For Ever, continued
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 14:20
fig: Hebrews 7:7 | Hebrews 7:7 | His use by Paul as an example of a non-Levitical priest, to whom tithes and honour are due: that he was a pattern of Jesus and the priesthood of believers of Christ Himself. Showing that the less was blessed by the greater.
fig: Hebrews 7:17 | Hebrews 7:17
refs: Hebrews 7:4-17

--- A Priest For Ever, continued
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 14:18
fig: Hebrews 7:24-25 | Hebrews 7:24-25
fig: Hebrews 7:26-27 | Hebrews 7:26-27

--- Jesus in the Old Testament
kicker: A theophany • Genesis 14:18
sub: Made Like unto the Son of God
p: The appearance of Jesus throughout the Old Testament, when the {Lord} speaks in person to the patriarchs, is called a “theophany,” and most believers see Jesus in the “angel of God” passages throughout Genesis: the {Lord} who meets with and speaks to them, who appears to stop Abraham’s hand sacrificing Isaac, who wrestles with Jacob, who appears in the fiery furnace in Daniel. As the Bible itself is one story, telling us about God, Jesus, the Holy Ghost and the Godhead’s relationship with man, Jesus appears in every book of the Old Testament, regardless of manifestation. [[Genesis 22:11-12]] [[Genesis 32:24-30]] [[Daniel 3:25]]
callout: Here, Melchizedek is seen as an early appearance of Jesus: the king of peace, the priest of the high God. Without a beginning or end, no mother or father given, *made like unto the Son of God; abideth a priest continually*. I believe it is Jesus, personally. But either way, it’s a sign that the ordinance of bread and wine, of faith in God as Saviour, as the faith of the patriarchs, was always about Jesus.

=== The spoils of Sodom
--- Not a Thread nor a Shoelatchet
kicker: vv. 21–24
sub: Abram Refuses the Spoils of Sodom
scripture: Genesis 14:21-24 | mark: I will not take from a thread even to a shoelatchet
p: Abram refuses spoils from the king of Sodom, not wanting anything from that wicked man. He did what he did because it was right. Abram rejects the treasure, but ensures a tenth of the spoils were given to Melchizedek as an offering to God. Because all things are God’s.

--- What Saved Believers Look Like
center: This should be a picture of what action of saved believers looks like: staying out of the war, not getting entangled in commitments with the wicked, not seeking treasure, but defending brethren from violence, and having faith in God to destroy our enemies when we do defend the righteous.
